WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.798
This is the narrated briefing for
a companion guide in the Federal

3
00:00:04.798 --> 00:00:09.274
Modernization Workbook: Zero Trust
Assessment, an implementation guide.

4
00:00:09.274 --> 00:00:12.987
Microsoft's Zero Trust Assessment
tells you, test by test,

5
00:00:12.987 --> 00:00:17.963
how a tenant's configuration compares
with Microsoft's Zero Trust guidance.

6
00:00:17.963 --> 00:00:20.041
It doesn't tell you what to fix first,

7
00:00:20.041 --> 00:00:23.124
what a skipped test means,
or when a finding is closed.

8
00:00:23.124 --> 00:00:26.388
Those are the program's to
decide, and the guide is how.

9
00:00:26.388 --> 00:00:31.712
Its facts come from Microsoft Learn's
three Zero Trust Assessment pages and the

10
00:00:31.712 --> 00:00:35.826
tool's own source and guide at
release three point one point oh,

11
00:00:35.826 --> 00:00:37.924
read on November eleventh, 2026.

12
00:00:37.924 --> 00:00:40.838
The program's own rules
are called design guidance.

13
00:00:40.838 --> 00:00:43.862
Nothing in the guide was
run against a live tenant.

14
00:00:43.862 --> 00:00:47.720
We'll go in four parts:
why, what, who, and how.

15
00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:55.608
Here's the idea the guide is built
on: a score is not a verdict.

16
00:00:55.608 --> 00:00:59.171
The report compares each setting
with Microsoft's guidance,

17
00:00:59.171 --> 00:01:01.808
and it reads configuration
on the day it runs.

18
00:01:01.808 --> 00:01:04.297
It can't tell you which
finding to fix first,

19
00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:07.357
what a skipped test means,
or when a finding is closed.

20
00:01:07.357 --> 00:01:09.936
Those decisions belong to named people,

21
00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:12.983
and the report is the
evidence they decide with,

22
00:01:12.983 --> 00:01:18.296
as the Zero Trust and Assessment book argues
in its chapter, Evidence, Not Verdicts.

23
00:01:18.296 --> 00:01:22.480
So every run is scoped, authorized
and pinned to a tool version,

24
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:25.270
and its output stays inside the boundary.

25
00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:30.095
Every result is recorded twice, the
tool's value and a normalized one.

26
00:01:30.095 --> 00:01:34.088
Every finding gets an owner, a route
to the page that remediates it,

27
00:01:34.088 --> 00:01:36.233
and a weighted place in the backlog.

28
00:01:36.233 --> 00:01:39.120
Every run is compared
with the last and sealed.

29
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.734
A finding is ready for closure when
every applicable state has evidence and

30
00:01:43.734 --> 00:01:48.860
a reassessment shows a pass; the authorizing
official or the assessor decides.

31
00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:53.635
What does it check?

32
00:01:53.635 --> 00:01:57.715
Microsoft Learn says it tests
hundreds of items from Microsoft's

33
00:01:57.715 --> 00:02:02.248
configuration guidance for Entra,
Intune, Azure networking and Purview.

34
00:02:02.248 --> 00:02:08.249
Release three point one point oh holds
three hundred sixty-four tests across seven

35
00:02:08.249 --> 00:02:11.118
pillars: identity, devices, network, data,

36
00:02:11.118 --> 00:02:14.336
infrastructure, security
operations, and A I.

37
00:02:14.336 --> 00:02:16.447
The guide names four things it isn't.

38
00:02:16.447 --> 00:02:20.235
It isn't a verdict: a pass
rate isn't a maturity stage.

39
00:02:20.235 --> 00:02:23.760
It isn't a substitute for
ScubaGear, which comes next.

40
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.760
It isn't a supported product: its read me
calls it a community project maintained

41
00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:33.685
by Microsoft employees, provided as is,
outside any Microsoft support program.

42
00:02:33.685 --> 00:02:37.429
And it isn't a detector: Learn's
Global Secure Access guidance

43
00:02:37.429 --> 00:02:41.398
calls it a configuration-posture
check, to be paired with alerts.

44
00:02:41.398 --> 00:02:43.260
In federal terms it's optional.

45
00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:46.077
The program runs it as
risk-based recommended,

46
00:02:46.077 --> 00:02:48.880
for the breadth ScubaGear doesn't cover.

47
00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:55.680
So run it alongside
ScubaGear, never instead.

48
00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:00.361
CISA's directive B O D twenty-five
oh-one names CISA's SCuBA tools,

49
00:03:00.361 --> 00:03:03.655
and the Zero Trust Assessment
isn't one of them.

50
00:03:03.655 --> 00:03:08.152
ScubaGear covers Exchange Online,
Teams, SharePoint and Power Platform,

51
00:03:08.152 --> 00:03:10.924
which the Zero Trust
Assessment doesn't test,

52
00:03:10.924 --> 00:03:15.571
and the Zero Trust and Assessment book
counts twenty-two of the directive's

53
00:03:15.571 --> 00:03:20.367
fifty mandatory Microsoft three sixty-five
policies in those four products.

54
00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:24.030
The Zero Trust Assessment
brings breadth instead:

55
00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:27.430
devices, network, data
and security operations.

56
00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:32.210
The two overlap mostly on Entra I D
identity and email threat policies,

57
00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:34.930
and a shared area isn't a duplicate check.

58
00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:36.729
When both fail the same setting,

59
00:03:36.729 --> 00:03:41.226
the ScubaGear finding is the directive's
deviation and carries its reporting;

60
00:03:41.226 --> 00:03:45.793
the Zero Trust Assessment finding is
supporting evidence for the same control.

61
00:03:45.793 --> 00:03:48.370
Nothing in this guide reports to CISA.

62
00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:57.170
Here's the path on one page, each step
with a done-when and a way to validate it.

63
00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:01.520
One: decide and scope, until
the decision record is approved.

64
00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.720
Two: a clean host with
PowerShell seven point six,

65
00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.920
the pinned module, and a
protected report folder.

66
00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:13.482
Three: consent once, give the run account
read roles only, and review the grants.

67
00:04:13.482 --> 00:04:17.146
Four: connect every service
in the tenant's own cloud.

68
00:04:17.146 --> 00:04:19.920
Five: run it, and hash the export.

69
00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:24.282
Six: pillar owners read their
tabs and note skips and errors.

70
00:04:24.282 --> 00:04:26.894
Seven: normalize every result.

71
00:04:26.894 --> 00:04:31.583
Eight: register every finding with
an owner, a family and a page.

72
00:04:31.583 --> 00:04:36.195
Nine: reconcile overlapping findings
through the setting and the control.

73
00:04:36.195 --> 00:04:40.783
Ten: re-run on a schedule,
compare, and seal the evidence.

74
00:04:40.783 --> 00:04:43.696
Steps seven to ten run
the guide's results kit,

75
00:04:43.696 --> 00:04:47.450
which reads files only and
never touches the tenant.

76
00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:52.925
Does it work in a government cloud?

77
00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:56.938
Microsoft Learn's three Zero
Trust Assessment pages don't say.

78
00:04:56.938 --> 00:05:02.369
The tool's three point one point oh
source does: its connect command takes an

79
00:05:02.369 --> 00:05:07.625
environment, Global, U S Gov or U S Gov
D O D, and an Exchange environment name.

80
00:05:07.625 --> 00:05:11.313
Each service it signs in to is
documented per cloud on Learn,

81
00:05:11.313 --> 00:05:14.925
and the guide matches the tool's
parameters to those pages.

82
00:05:14.925 --> 00:05:19.609
One place they conflict: for Security
and Compliance in D O D, Learn gives

83
00:05:19.609 --> 00:05:22.308
compliance dot D O D
dot microsoft dot com,

84
00:05:22.308 --> 00:05:25.325
and the tool's source
uses a different address.

85
00:05:25.325 --> 00:05:27.113
Which one works wasn't tested.

86
00:05:27.113 --> 00:05:32.148
For SharePoint, the tool infers a
sharepoint dot U S admin address,

87
00:05:32.148 --> 00:05:35.412
so pass it explicitly;
D O D's isn't specified.

88
00:05:35.412 --> 00:05:39.581
Until a run is proved in your cloud,
run one pillar at a time in a test

89
00:05:39.581 --> 00:05:44.560
tenant of the same cloud, and keep
ScubaGear as the federal baseline tool.

90
00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:49.148
Now, who.

91
00:05:49.148 --> 00:05:53.972
The first connection asks an administrator
to consent to the tool's Microsoft

92
00:05:53.972 --> 00:05:58.797
Graph read permissions; later runs don't
ask again unless a new version adds one.

93
00:05:58.797 --> 00:06:00.434
The sources differ on who.

94
00:06:00.434 --> 00:06:02.522
Learn says a Global Administrator.

95
00:06:02.522 --> 00:06:07.462
The tool's guide for three point one point
oh says a Cloud Application Administrator

96
00:06:07.462 --> 00:06:11.697
or Application Administrator, and
that consent assigns no runtime role.

97
00:06:11.697 --> 00:06:16.723
Learn lists twenty-one permissions; the
source requests thirty-four, all read.

98
00:06:16.723 --> 00:06:19.010
The program follows the tool's guide.

99
00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:22.997
The consent administrator consents
and doesn't run the assessment.

100
00:06:22.997 --> 00:06:27.510
The run account is a dedicated reader
whose roles are activated through pim

101
00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:32.309
before each run; SharePoint Administrator
is the one write-capable role it needs.

102
00:06:32.309 --> 00:06:36.702
Consent lands on the shared Graph
PowerShell application by default,

103
00:06:36.702 --> 00:06:38.898
so the program uses a dedicated one.

104
00:06:38.898 --> 00:06:43.810
The guide's RACI names who is responsible
and accountable for each activity.

105
00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:49.723
Now how, starting with the host.

106
00:06:49.723 --> 00:06:53.972
The report and export hold tenant
information an attacker could use.

107
00:06:53.972 --> 00:06:58.847
Learn says to share them only with authorized
personnel, store the report securely,

108
00:06:58.847 --> 00:07:02.661
and delete the generated folder
when the assessment is complete.

109
00:07:02.661 --> 00:07:06.886
The tool's guide adds that exports and
logs can hold personal information,

110
00:07:06.886 --> 00:07:11.598
and that deleting the report folder doesn't
remove the PowerShell logging folder.

111
00:07:11.598 --> 00:07:16.509
So open a clean PowerShell session on
a dedicated host inside the boundary.

112
00:07:16.509 --> 00:07:21.206
Learn says PowerShell seven; the
tool's guide asks for seven point six,

113
00:07:21.206 --> 00:07:23.348
for its pinned Exchange module.

114
00:07:23.348 --> 00:07:27.047
Install and pin the version
the decision record names.

115
00:07:27.047 --> 00:07:30.189
Create a new, empty report
folder for each run,

116
00:07:30.189 --> 00:07:34.348
restricted to the run account
and the evidence custodians.

117
00:07:34.348 --> 00:07:39.109
In step ten, the unaltered export
moves into the evidence repository,

118
00:07:39.109 --> 00:07:42.890
the copy is hashed, and the
local folder is deleted.

119
00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:48.040
Step four connects.

120
00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:52.537
The tool signs in separately to
Microsoft Graph, Azure, Exchange Online,

121
00:07:52.537 --> 00:07:57.567
Security and Compliance, SharePoint
Online and Azure Information Protection,

122
00:07:57.567 --> 00:08:00.540
and a Graph connection
doesn't grant the others.

123
00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:04.053
Learn's get-started page shows
only the commercial command,

124
00:08:04.053 --> 00:08:07.852
so the guide takes each cloud's
parameters from the tool's source.

125
00:08:07.852 --> 00:08:11.302
Commercial and G C C use
the Global environment.

126
00:08:11.302 --> 00:08:16.392
G C C High uses U S Gov with
its Exchange environment name,

127
00:08:16.392 --> 00:08:19.490
and D O D uses U S Gov D O D with its own.

128
00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:24.990
Then confirm the tenant and cloud with
Get M G Context and Get A Z Context.

129
00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:30.165
Pass the SharePoint admin U R L in
every cloud, or the tool infers one.

130
00:08:30.165 --> 00:08:33.314
If a service won't connect,
connect the rest, run,

131
00:08:33.314 --> 00:08:37.540
and record its tests as skipped,
disconnected, with the reason.

132
00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:41.096
In D O D, if the Purview
tests skip as disconnected,

133
00:08:41.096 --> 00:08:44.200
check the conflicting address first.

134
00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:49.250
Step five runs it.

135
00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:52.950
The assessment is read-only
and stores its data locally.

136
00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:56.750
On a large tenant it can take
more than twenty-four hours,

137
00:08:56.750 --> 00:09:00.962
and Learn says not to stop it, even
if it logs warnings or errors.

138
00:09:00.962 --> 00:09:05.385
The disable-telemetry switch
suppresses the one telemetry event,

139
00:09:05.385 --> 00:09:09.325
which carries the tenant I D;
using it is the see-so's decision.

140
00:09:09.325 --> 00:09:12.900
The no-browser switch keeps
the report off a shared screen.

141
00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:18.653
As soon as the run finishes, before
anyone opens or copies anything,

142
00:09:18.653 --> 00:09:23.988
hash the export, S H A two fifty-six,
and hash every file into a C S V.

143
00:09:23.988 --> 00:09:26.687
Those hashes show the
output wasn't modified.

144
00:09:26.687 --> 00:09:30.505
If the run fails, capture a support
package in the same session,

145
00:09:30.505 --> 00:09:32.451
to a separate protected folder.

146
00:09:32.451 --> 00:09:36.532
Everything under the zt-export
folder, including the jay-son

147
00:09:36.532 --> 00:09:40.810
report data the kit reads, is
assessment data, not just logs.

148
00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:47.498
Steps six and seven read the results.

149
00:09:47.498 --> 00:09:49.547
Each pillar owner reads their tab.

150
00:09:49.547 --> 00:09:53.278
Every test ends in one
of six statuses: passed,

151
00:09:53.278 --> 00:09:57.109
failed, investigate,
skipped, planned, or error.

152
00:09:57.109 --> 00:09:59.622
Investigate means a person needs to look,

153
00:09:59.622 --> 00:10:03.685
and skipped carries a reason, such
as not licensed or not connected.

154
00:10:03.685 --> 00:10:07.418
The kit records every result
twice: the tool's value,

155
00:10:07.418 --> 00:10:10.885
unchanged, and one of
seven normalized results.

156
00:10:10.885 --> 00:10:12.573
That's the rule in the title.

157
00:10:12.573 --> 00:10:16.247
A skipped test is not a failed
control, and it's not a pass.

158
00:10:16.247 --> 00:10:22.086
It goes in its own column, with its reason:
a licensing decision, or a run to fix.

159
00:10:22.086 --> 00:10:27.510
Investigate counts as a finding until a
recorded review confirms or dismisses it.

160
00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:28.923
Two federal cases.

161
00:10:28.923 --> 00:10:32.103
Not dot gov domain
usually means the agency's

162
00:10:32.103 --> 00:10:35.111
dot gov domain isn't
verified in the tenant.

163
00:10:35.111 --> 00:10:40.412
And Learn says impersonation protection
isn't yet available in G C C High and D O D,

164
00:10:40.412 --> 00:10:44.710
so a failed impersonation test
there is a boundary finding.

165
00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:51.035
Step eight opens the closure register.

166
00:10:51.035 --> 00:10:55.153
Every finding gets one entry
with the closure chain's fields,

167
00:10:55.153 --> 00:10:59.848
from the Zero Trust and Assessment
book: the finding and tool version,

168
00:10:59.848 --> 00:11:03.719
the attack path, the owner,
dependencies, the change, pilot,

169
00:11:03.719 --> 00:11:09.073
enforcement and test, the evidence,
residual risk, reassessment and weighting.

170
00:11:09.073 --> 00:11:12.399
Each finding family routes to
the page that remediates it,

171
00:11:12.399 --> 00:11:15.310
with the evidence that shows
it's ready for closure.

172
00:11:15.310 --> 00:11:18.503
Emergency access goes to
Access and Privilege,

173
00:11:18.503 --> 00:11:21.122
with a test sign-in that fired its alert.

174
00:11:21.122 --> 00:11:24.499
Conditional Access gaps
go to its policy library,

175
00:11:24.499 --> 00:11:27.135
with a test sign-in blocked as designed.

176
00:11:27.135 --> 00:11:30.097
Skipped tests go to step
seven's dispositions.

177
00:11:30.097 --> 00:11:33.935
A finding with no family goes
to the Zero Trust program lead.

178
00:11:33.935 --> 00:11:38.110
The kit sets a default priority,
and the owner writes the rationale.

179
00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:41.560
An exception with an expiry
is the see-so's decision;

180
00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:44.435
one without is the authorizing official's.

181
00:11:44.435 --> 00:11:47.650
The kit never marks a
finding ready for closure.

182
00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:53.063
Step nine reconciles.

183
00:11:53.063 --> 00:11:56.527
Tools with different identifiers
can't be matched directly,

184
00:11:56.527 --> 00:12:00.686
so the program matches each to a common
spine: the setting, and the control

185
00:12:00.686 --> 00:12:04.775
it supports in the NIST special
publication eight hundred fifty-three.

186
00:12:04.775 --> 00:12:09.414
Two tools failing the same setting
under the same control are one finding,

187
00:12:09.414 --> 00:12:12.000
with two pieces of evidence and one owner.

188
00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:17.250
CISA maps every SCuBA policy to those
controls in the fed-ramp High baseline.

189
00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:22.168
On AWS, Security Hub's standard for that
publication supports a subset of it,

190
00:12:22.168 --> 00:12:23.992
with automated checks only,

191
00:12:23.992 --> 00:12:28.275
and each finding lists its related
requirements: that's the spine.

192
00:12:28.275 --> 00:12:33.117
Its statuses map to the taxonomy, and
A W S warns that a control with no

193
00:12:33.117 --> 00:12:38.738
resources passes at the account level, so
a pass can mean nothing was evaluated.

194
00:12:38.738 --> 00:12:43.069
On the other clouds, Oracle Cloud
Infrastructure's Cloud Guard and Google

195
00:12:43.069 --> 00:12:48.390
Cloud's Security Command Center are compared
in Security Operations and Telemetry.

196
00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:54.227
Step ten makes it repeatable.

197
00:12:54.227 --> 00:12:57.733
The kit compares each
run with the last: new,

198
00:12:57.733 --> 00:13:02.040
regressed, fixed, still open,
and no longer evaluated.

199
00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:04.028
That last class matters most.

200
00:13:04.028 --> 00:13:08.991
A finding the tool skipped this time
isn't fixed: fix the run and run it again.

201
00:13:08.991 --> 00:13:11.684
Runs from different tool
versions are refused

202
00:13:11.684 --> 00:13:14.240
until someone records
a review of the change.

203
00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:19.180
Then seal the evidence: each output
folder becomes a chained record with its

204
00:13:19.180 --> 00:13:22.865
controls, every file's hash,
and the previous record's hash,

205
00:13:22.865 --> 00:13:25.453
which the I S S O can
check without the kit.

206
00:13:25.453 --> 00:13:31.026
The cadence is design guidance from the
day-two runbook in Compliance Automation and

207
00:13:31.026 --> 00:13:34.560
Day-2 Operations: monthly,
after each remediation wave,

208
00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:36.915
before a tool upgrade, and quarterly.

209
00:13:36.915 --> 00:13:41.917
Learn's weekly digest playbook comes with
two cautions: report counts, not its

210
00:13:41.917 --> 00:13:47.520
pass rate, and whether it runs against three
point one point oh isn't yet specified.

211
00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:53.133
To close, the dangers.

212
00:13:53.133 --> 00:13:56.258
Counting a skipped test
as a failure or a pass.

213
00:13:56.258 --> 00:13:59.344
Calling a finding fixed because
the next run skipped it.

214
00:13:59.344 --> 00:14:01.945
Treating the pass rate
as a maturity score.

215
00:14:01.945 --> 00:14:05.307
Running the Zero Trust
Assessment instead of ScubaGear.

216
00:14:05.307 --> 00:14:10.571
Global Administrator for every run, or
consent on the shared Graph application.

217
00:14:10.571 --> 00:14:14.270
And the wrong cloud, or a
report folder left on the host.

218
00:14:14.270 --> 00:14:16.107
Each has its guard in the steps.

219
00:14:16.107 --> 00:14:21.123
The guide is plain about what it couldn't
verify: government clouds on Learn,

220
00:14:21.123 --> 00:14:25.412
D O D's Security and Compliance
address and SharePoint admin U R L,

221
00:14:25.412 --> 00:14:29.619
which run roles apply, and Graph
permissions in G C C High and D O D.

222
00:14:29.619 --> 00:14:31.533
Nothing was run against a tenant.

223
00:14:31.533 --> 00:14:36.577
To practice, start the learning path,
The Zero Trust Assessment as Evidence:

224
00:14:36.577 --> 00:14:39.344
four modules, each with a knowledge check.

225
